## FAQ

### Q1: Which of these three majors has the highest starting salary?

Energy engineering typically offers the highest starting salary, with median annual wages for entry-level electrical engineers at approximately $75,000 in 2022, according to the U.S. Bureau of Labor Statistics. Materials science graduates start around $68,000, while environmental economists begin near $60,000. However, by mid-career, materials scientists who move into semiconductor or battery R&D often surpass their engineering peers, with top earners exceeding $160,000. The salary trajectory depends heavily on industry sector and geographic location.

### Q2: Can I switch between these majors after my first year?

Yes, but the cost varies. Energy engineering and materials science share foundational coursework in calculus, physics, and chemistry during the first year, so switching between them typically adds only one semester. Environmental economics requires different prerequisites—microeconomics and statistics—so switching into it from an engineering track may add two semesters. The reverse is harder: economics majors who switch into engineering often need to complete three semesters of calculus and physics, delaying graduation by a full year.

### Q3: Which major is best for working on solar energy specifically?

For solar hardware—panel design, cell efficiency, manufacturing—materials science is the strongest choice, given that perovskite research is advancing 33.7% efficiency cells at NREL. For solar installation and grid integration—designing solar farms, connecting them to transmission lines—energy engineering is better. For solar policy and incentives—designing tax credits, net metering rules, or renewable portfolio standards—environmental economics is the path. The International Energy Agency’s *Solar PV Global Supply Chains 2022* report noted that the solar workforce spans all three disciplines, with roughly 40% in engineering roles, 10% in materials R&D, and 15% in policy and economics.

